      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the volume of the inscribed sphere within a cube with a given edge length, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the relationship between the sphere and the cube:**\n   - An inscribed sphere means the sphere is tangent to all six faces of the cube.\n   - This implies that the diameter of the sphere is equal to the edge length of the cube.\n\n2. **Determine the edge length of the cube:**\n   - Given that the edge length of the cube is 6 inches, the diameter of the sphere is also 6 inches.\n   - Therefore, the radius \\( r \\) of the sphere is half of the diameter:\n     \\[\n     r = \\frac{\\text{diameter}}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\text{ inches}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the volume of the sphere:**\n   - The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a sphere is:\n     \\[\n     V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3\n     \\]\n   - Substitute the radius \\( r = 3 \\) inches into the formula:\n     \\[\n     V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (3)^3\n     \\]\n   - Compute \\( 3^3 \\):\n     \\[\n     3^3 = 27\n     \\]\n   - Substitute \\( 27 \\) into the volume formula:\n     \\[\n     V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\times 27\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the expression:\n     \\[\n     V = \\frac{4 \\times 27}{3} \\pi = \\frac{108}{3} \\pi = 36 \\pi\n     \\]\n\n4. **State the final answer:**\n   - The volume of the inscribed sphere is:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{36\\pi}\n     \\]",
